# Argon Atomic Diff Sequencer (`argon-atomic-diff-sequencer`)

Out of the box, large language models with massive context windows (such as Gemini 4 Argon) can default to rewriting entire multi-hundred line files when asked to fix a minor bug. Full-file overwrites (`write_to_file`) burn substantial output tokens ($10.00/1M tokens), introduce accidental comment deletions, and increase merge conflicts.

This skill equips Argon with Claude's signature atomic precision by enforcing a strict **surgical chunk replacement protocol**.

---

## 1. The 30-Line Atomic Diff Ceiling

- **The Rule:** All modifications to existing codebases must be performed via targeted chunk replacements (`replace_file_content`) with chunks **$\le$ 30 lines**.
- **Prohibited:** Overwriting entire existing files with `write_to_file` unless explicitly creating a brand-new file from scratch.
- **Context Anchor:** Always include 2–3 lines of surrounding immutable context in `TargetContent` to guarantee 100% unique match resolution.

---

## 2. Multi-Chunk Refactoring Pipeline

When refactoring multiple functions across a large file:
1. **Analyze First:** Read the exact line coordinates via `view_file` or `slice-reader`.
2. **Order of Application:** Apply chunk replacements sequentially from bottom-to-top or using unique semantic anchors to avoid line number drift.
3. **Preserve Surrounding Logic:** Never truncate or rewrite unrelated methods, comments, or docstrings.

---

## 3. Quota & Token Economics

| Editing Approach | Output Tokens Generated | Risk of Unrelated Code Deletion |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Full File Overwrite** (500-line file) | ~1,800 – 2,500 tokens | High (accidental regressions) |
| **Atomic Chunk Replacement** (15-line diff) | **~80 – 150 tokens** | **0% (Isolated surgical fix)** |
| **Net Savings on Argon** | **94% output quota saved** | **Claude-grade precision locked** |

